  "proof": [
    "The bounded matcher accepts exactly a conjunction of two ascending i64 cursor bounds, a two-arm integer selection/store/increment, and one output increment. Four integer element types (i32/u32/i64/u64), exact contextual element types and distinct private unaddressed/uncaptured/nonreusable local cursors are required. Bound endpoints cannot alias a modified cursor.",
    "A successful source guard forces 0<=begin<exclusive_end<=packed_u32_length. Thus valid differences are positive u32-bounded, their sum fits u64, and all successful width multiplications and physical endpoint additions are exact. Raw arithmetic for invalid ranges may wrap but cannot authorize the fast path because the full conjunction rejects them.",
    "Destination position and total remaining counts must fit its logical suffix, then its widened complete physical span. This overestimates the actual writes before one source exhausts. Successful guards prove all admitted loads/stores and cursor increments nontrapping and nonwrapping; failed guards perform no payload access or write and retain scalar execution.",
    "The fast loop preserves original Truth(loop->cond), reads A then B, applies BinaryOp with the original comparator and exact signedness, stores select(A,B,predicate), then increments A by predicate, B by its inverse and output by one. Source and destination may overlap: no write or callback intervenes between original comparison loads and its redundant selected RHS reload.",
    "Captured pointers/views and bounds stay in private Wasm locals. No prefix-resolution call is possible under the mapping exclusion. The accepted body cannot allocate, call user code, materialize owners, exit nonlocally or expose cursor addresses; omitted block bookkeeping has no intervening observer or allocation.",
    "The original scalar fallback remains intact, with the rematch-suppression flag active throughout emission and reset afterward. The former global proven-index set and Address hook were removed. Debug and unsupported shapes retain scalar code."
  ],
